Concept: Hong Kong–Zhuhai Energy & HSR Barrage

The existing Hong Kong–Zhuhai–Macao Bridge is about 55 km overall, with approximately 42 km from the Hong Kong Port side to Zhuhai/Macao Port and a 29.6 km main bridge/tunnel section.

The proposal would create a new, parallel infrastructure corridor, rather than simply putting a railway onto the existing bridge.

Indicative corridor

Hong Kong → Lantau → Lingdingyang → Zhuhai

Component	Concept
Total barrage/corridor	~45–55 km
HSR design speed	400 km/h
Railway	2 tracks, electrified/H₂ rolling-stock concept
Tidal turbines	~0.5–1.0 GW initial installation
Potential basin	~1,000–1,700 km² depending on final hydrodynamic boundary
Indicative annual tidal generation	~0.75–1.3 TWh/year at 1.5 m effective range
Higher-head scenario	~1.3–2.3 TWh/year at 2 m
Major navigation channels	Large ship locks / gated navigation openings
Additional energy	Floating/roof solar + offshore wind + hydrogen production

Lingding Bay is approximately 1,700 km² in one major study, while other definitions of the wider Lingding system give areas of roughly 1,180–2,110 km². The Pearl River itself delivers roughly 330–350 billion m³ of freshwater annually into the estuary, making hydrodynamic modelling absolutely fundamental to the project.

The railway

The most interesting feature would be the 400 km/h H₂-HSR spine running directly along the barrage.

For the approximately 42 km Hong Kong Port–Zhuhai Port distance, the theoretical time at a constant 400 km/h is:

42 ÷ 400 × 60 = 6.3 minutes

But a real train cannot instantly reach 400 km/h.

With a representative acceleration/deceleration profile, the practical non-stop running time would be approximately:

8–10 minutes

For a ~50 km engineering alignment:

~10–12 minutes

For a ~55 km end-to-end corridor:

~11–13 minutes

So I would specify the infrastructure around a headline:

HONG KONG → ZHUHAI: ~10 MINUTES BY 400 KM/H HSR

rather than claiming that every journey would literally take 6 minutes.

For comparison, the existing HZMB reports about 40 minutes for the approximately 42 km Hong Kong Port–Zhuhai/Macao Port journey by road.

Why 400 km/h matters

The railway would be designed for extremely short inter-city trips:

Journey	Approx. rail distance	400 km/h theoretical	Practical target
Hong Kong–Zhuhai	~42–50 km	6–7.5 min	8–10 min
Hong Kong–Zhuhai, full corridor	~55 km	8.25 min	11–13 min
Hong Kong–Zhongshan	~55–70 km	8–10.5 min	11–15 min
Hong Kong–Shenzhen western corridor	~60–80 km	9–12 min	12–17 min
Hong Kong–Guangzhou	~130–150 km	19.5–22.5 min	23–30 min

These are engineering scenario calculations, not existing journey times or promised travel times.

The wider concept fits the region's existing direction toward additional cross-boundary rail. Hong Kong and Shenzhen are already developing the Hong Kong–Shenzhen Western Rail Link, with the Hong Kong government targeting construction completion in 2034 and commissioning in 2035.

1. The barrage

The barrage would essentially become a multi-purpose artificial infrastructure island/viaduct.

Cross-section:

400 km/h HSR

↓

Maintenance / emergency deck

↓

Structural barrage / causeway

↓

Tidal sluices + reversible bulb turbines

↓

Navigation channels

↓

Pearl River Estuary

Instead of attempting to dam every channel solidly, the structure would have large controlled openings at the principal navigation and ecological channels.

The Pearl River Estuary has complex bathymetry, with deep navigation channels and extensive shallow shoals. One study identifies depths of up to roughly 20 m in the main western channel while much of the surrounding area is shallower.

That makes a conventional solid wall across the entire estuary inappropriate.

2. Tidal power

The basic tidal-barrage relationship is:

E ≈ ½ AρgH²

where:

A = impounded area
ρ = seawater density
g = gravity
H = tidal head

The Hong Kong Electrical and Mechanical Services Department describes the same fundamental principle: larger impounded area and larger tidal head increase available energy, while generation can operate on ebb, flood, or two-way cycles.

Conservative scenario

Assume:

basin = 1,000 km²
effective tidal range = 1.5 m
turbine/system efficiency = ~33%

Approximate annual output:

~0.75 TWh/year

Large-basin scenario

Assume:

basin = 1,700 km²
effective tidal range = 1.5 m
efficiency = ~33%

Approximate annual output:

~1.3 TWh/year

Higher-head scenario

If engineering works could achieve an effective 2 m generation head:

~2.3 TWh/year

The quadratic relationship with tidal range is important: doubling the head theoretically quadruples the available energy. But that should not be interpreted as saying the entire natural 2–3 m tidal range could simply be converted into electricity; the actual output would require full hydrodynamic modelling.

For context, modelling of tidal-current resources in the Pearl River Estuary has found relatively low annual-average tidal-current power density—generally below 0.10 kW/m²—reinforcing why a barrage would need a very large basin to produce substantial electricity.

3. Installed generating capacity

I'd therefore design the first phase around:

600–1,000 MW tidal generating capacity

rather than claiming several gigawatts of continuous output.

The turbines would operate intensely around the tidal generation windows and then shut down between generation periods.

That produces a useful combination:

Large installed power

→ short periods of high output

→ predictable twice-daily generation

→ grid balancing

→ hydrogen production during favourable periods.

The electricity could feed:

HSR traction
Hong Kong/Zhuhai grid
electrolytic hydrogen
desalination
industrial loads
battery storage
data centres
port operations.
4. Hydrogen system

The HSR shouldn't necessarily depend on hydrogen for every metre of its operation.

A more technically interesting architecture would be:

Tidal electricity

→ grid + electrolyser

→ green hydrogen

→ H₂ refuelling/storage

→ hydrogen HSR fleet

with the railway also able to draw electricity from the grid.

That gives the railway redundancy.

Hydrogen production could be concentrated at large energy hubs at:

Hong Kong Energy Island → Barrage → Zhuhai Energy Island

with high-pressure hydrogen storage adjacent to stations and maintenance depots.

The railway could consequently become part of an integrated:

Tidal power + hydrogen + high-speed transport system

rather than simply a railway sitting on a dam.

5. Navigation

This is one of the project's largest engineering problems.

The Pearl River Delta is one of the world's major port regions. A barrage cannot simply obstruct shipping.

I would therefore specify three major navigation gates:

Eastern navigation channel

Hong Kong/Lantau shipping routes.

Central navigation channel

Deep-draught Lingdingyang traffic.

Western navigation channel

Zhuhai/Macao/West Pearl River shipping.

Each would have:

ship locks or tidal gates
deep-water navigation channel
redundant gate systems
radar/AIS integration
automated marine traffic control.

The barrage would effectively become a controlled hydraulic boundary with permanent maritime corridors.

6. Flood-control function

There is potentially another major infrastructure function.

Because the Pearl River carries approximately 330–350 billion m³ of freshwater annually, and wet-season discharge can be several times dry-season discharge, the structure would have to be designed primarily as a hydraulic-management system rather than merely a power station.

During extreme rainfall:

River discharge increases

→ barrage gates open

→ floodwater passes through

→ navigation restrictions activate

→ turbines can operate where hydraulic conditions permit.

The system should never be designed around maximising electricity at the expense of flood conveyance.

7. The HSR architecture

I'd make the railway four-track rather than two-track if the goal is genuinely to transform the Pearl River Delta.

Four-track configuration

Track 1: Hong Kong → Zhuhai
Track 2: Zhuhai → Hong Kong
Track 3: express / limited-stop
Track 4: regional stopping service

This permits a mixture of:

400 km/h express

and

200–300 km/h regional services

without stopping the express trains behind local services.

Stations could include:

Hong Kong
→ Lantau
→ artificial-island energy hub
→ western estuary
→ Zhuhai

with onward branches toward:

Macau / Zhongshan / Jiangmen / Guangzhou

and eventually integration with the wider Greater Bay Area rail network.

8. The real strategic concept

The most interesting version isn't simply:

"Build a bridge with a train on it."

It is:

THE PEARL RIVER ENERGY & MOBILITY BARRAGE

One enormous piece of infrastructure combining:

400 km/h HSR

tidal electricity

green hydrogen

flood management

marine navigation

grid storage

cross-border transport

industrial energy

The result would turn the existing sea-crossing concept into a regional infrastructure platform.

The existing HZMB already demonstrates that a ~55 km Hong Kong–Zhuhai/Macao sea-crossing is technically achievable; the proposed scheme would be a much more ambitious second-generation concept, with the railway and energy infrastructure designed together from the beginning.

