Most importantly: the 1–2+ TWh/year figures are preliminary physics-based scenarios, not a bankable energy forecast. A real project would require a 2D/3D hydrodynamic model covering tides, river discharge, sediment transport, salinity, navigation, ecology and sea-level rise before a final basin area or turbine capacity could be specified. This is especially important because Lingding Bay is a highly modified estuarine system and receives a substantial share of Pearl River runoff and sediment.

A next-stage engineering concept would therefore be a ~50 km Hong Kong–Zhuhai Energy Bridge, with a 400 km/h HSR deck, 600–1,000 MW tidal turbine complex, hydrogen production, deep-water navigation gates and major artificial energy islands.
